#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065096} :activity/id #uuid "22d1805e-71b9-5243-96d1-5454b5d68ac6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050299 :topic/id #uuid "3d9b9134-f2c7-559d-b631-25a1da8ede8d" :topic/math-academy-id 338} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065438 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}\\,\\textrm d x\\,?$\n\nWe need to write the integrand so that it's something we can integrate. One way to do this is to divide $3x+2$ by $x+2$ using polynomial or synthetic division. We'll use synthetic division because it's faster. \n\nTo rewrite $\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2},$ we complete a synthetic division table.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-2$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ |\n\nThis tells us that $\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}$ can be decomposed into a quotient and remainder as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}= 3 - \\dfrac{4}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can integrate the function, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3-\\frac{4}{x+2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x-4\\operatorname{ln}|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2437a8a0-d1de-5b85-8577-7e7493c4d520" :tutorial/math-academy-id 884 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "83b3796b-b6b4-589d-a5b3-e83dd146da2d" :step/math-academy-id 1707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065102}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064096 :question/id #uuid "4d4edfc8-e178-58b4-b827-e4310ead130e" :question/math-academy-id "e-485"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "236f51c2-22ad-551f-afb4-62fd6de6d21e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592